es with one vertex identified.\n\n\nVolume96, Issue4\n\nMarch 2021\n\nPages 554-577\n\nChen G, Chen Y, Cui Q, Feng X, Liu Q. The chromatic number of {, diamond, bowtie}-free graphs. J Graph Theory. 2021; 96: 554–577. https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.22631\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRealizations of Multiassociahedra via Rigidity | Discrete & Computational Geometry | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00454-024-00698-y)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; It turns out that when this is done k-triangulations are related to fillings of certain polyominos and to bipartite rigidity theory in dimension k (instead of 2k); for example, readers can easily convince themselves that bipartized triangulations become spanning trees, which are the bases of 1-dimensional rigidity. ... As b is a side of the intersection, the triangle formed by b, d and e is inside the big half-planes of d and e, so we can again apply Lemma 3.14 to get that b has opposite sign to a and c, as we wanted to prove. \\(\\square \\) ... To prove Theorem 1.5 (that is, Conjecture 1.4 for \\(k=2\\)) we need operations that send a 2-triangulation in n vertices to one in \\(n+1\\) vertices, and vice versa. ... Again, we can flatten S, assume by the inductive hypothesis that the sign condition in part (3) of Corollary 2.14 holds in the flattened 5-cycle, and return to \\(n+1\\) vertices by a vertex split that will preserve the sign condition if the split vertices are kept close enough. \\(\\square \\) ... This reduces the number of variables in the feasibility problem from \\(n(n-2k-1)/2\\) to \\((n-2k)(n-2k-1)/2\\), a very significant reduction for the values of (n, k) where we can computationally construct \\(\\overline{\\Delta }_{k}(n)\\).\n### Remark 1.10\n\nTheorem 1.9 still leaves open the possibility of realizing \\(\\overline{\\Delta }_{k}(n)\\) for \\(n\\ge 2k+6 \\ge 12\\) via